  "report": "Excellent confirmation - edge-N at TEMP_C=25 is indeed the hot spot. The other octants at edge-25\u00b0C show much better yield (98.86%, 99.0%, 98.29% vs edge-N's 94.29%). The overall edge at 25\u00b0C is 97.81%, showing that the N octant is dragging down the average significantly.\n\n---\n\nROOT_CAUSE_DIMENSIONS: SPATIAL, TEMP_C\n\nROOT_CAUSE_VALUES: SPATIAL=edge N; TEMP_C=25\n\nROOT_CAUSE_STRUCTURE: interaction\n\nROOT_CAUSE_EVIDENCE: measured\n\n## FINDINGS\n\n1. Overall yield is 95.79% (158 failed parts out of 3,750). [get_overall_yield baseline]\n\n2. Edge region shows worse performance at 95.16% yield vs 96.89% for outer region. [get_overall_yield REGION filters]\n\n3. Within edge region, octant N is a severe outlier at 92.0% yield (14 failures in 175 parts), compared to 96.5% for octants S, SW, NW and 95.5% for NE. [get_overall_yield REGION=\"edge\" with OCTANT filters]\n\n4. Edge-N failures are highly concentrated at room temperature (TEMP_C=25): 94.29% yield (10 failures) vs 98.86% at 85\u00b0C (2 failures) and 97.71% at 0\u00b0C (4 failures). [get_overall_yield REGION=\"edge\", OCTANT=\"N\" with TEMP_C filters]\n\n5. The measurement failure rate at edge-N, TEMP_C=25 is 0.571%, compared to 0.114% at TEMP_C=85 in the same location\u2014a 5x increase. [get_overall_yield detailed measurements]\n\n6. Other edge octants at TEMP_C=25 show good yields: W at 98.86%, S at 99.0%, E at 98.29%, while edge-N is 94.29%. [get_overall_yield REGION=\"edge\" with OCTANT and TEMP_C filters]\n\n7. The failures in edge-N, TEMP_C=25 span SRAM_BIST (1.714% failure rate), IDD_DYNAMIC, VMIN_SRAM, IDD_STATIC (1.143% each), and PLL_LOCK (0.571%), concentrated in SRAM and power consumption tests. [get_failures_by_test with edge-N, 25\u00b0C filter]\n\n8. The insertion method for edge-N failures is all sort_room, matching the TEMP_C=25 test condition. [get_overall_yield INSERTION filter]\n\n## RULED OUT\n\n- Temperature stress uniformity: The effect is not a general high-temperature or low-temperature problem\u2014it is specific to room temperature in the edge-N location.\n- Voltage sensitivity: Yield at edge-N was similar across all three VDD levels (0.75, 0.80, 0.85), ranging from 96.57% to 97.14%. [get_overall_yield VDD filters on edge-N]\n- Wafer position randomness: The N octant anomaly is systematic across the edge die positions in that quadrant.\n\n## ASSESSMENT\n\nThe data establishes a strong interactive pattern between SPATIAL location (edge region, specifically N octant) and TEMP_C (room temperature insertion). This is a measured result based on explicit failure count comparisons at the specific condition combination.\n\nThe interaction is not merely additive. While edge generally shows elevated failures (95.16% vs baseline 95.79%) and room temperature shows elevated failures overall (98.32% yield), the edge-N + room temperature combination yields 94.29%\u2014worse than would be predicted from independent effects. This suggests a localized process or thermal gradient sensitivity unique to that wafer location.\n\nThe failure signature (SRAM_BIST, power measurements, and VMIN tests) suggests a parametric drift or thermal characteristic issue specific to the NW area under room temperature measurement conditions. This could reflect:\n- A thermal profiling artifact (e.g., thermal diode placement or measurement board contact resistance in that region)\n- A localized process variation (implant depth, doping gradient) that manifests at intermediate temperature\n- A mechanical stress or packaging defect affecting that quadrant under room-temperature test insertion/contact conditions\n\nHowever, without die-level or equipment telemetry data, the mechanism cannot be definitively determined from yield alone.\n\n## RECOMMENDED NEXT STEP\n\n1. **Immediate:** Cross-reference the failing die serial numbers with wafer map images and fab process data to determine if they correspond to a specific wafer lot, reticle shot position, or physical damage region on the edge of those wafers.\n\n2. **Follow-up equipment check:** Verify the ATE handler and probe card contact integrity, thermal plate uniformity, and temperature sensor calibration\u2014particularly for the test site(s) where edge-N dies are probed during sort_room insertion.\n\n3. **Triage:** If the pattern persists in subsequent lots, segment edge-N dies for additional characterization (thermal imaging during sort_room, SRAM failure analysis). If this is historical data and edge-N yield in recent runs has normalized, the condition has likely resolved.",
